# Arthritis Support App - Cursor Rules

This project is a mobile-first Arthritis Support App MVP, initially for one patient: the user's mom.

Goal: help her understand pain patterns and make better daily decisions with minimal effort.

The app should focus on:

- fast daily logging
- joint symptom tracking
- simple timeline visibility
- lightweight, rule-based insights
- gentle non-medical nudges

Do not build a generic medical platform yet.

## Product Rules

- Single-patient MVP first.
- Everything should be usable in under 30 seconds.
- Keep screens simple and calm.
- Avoid over-engineering.
- No machine learning in the MVP.
- No complex auth unless explicitly requested.
- No doctor dashboard unless explicitly requested.
- No medical diagnosis or medication instructions.

## Tech Direction

- Frontend: Expo, React Native, TypeScript.
- Backend direction: FastAPI, PostgreSQL, REST API.
- Use mock services when backend is not available.
- Keep API functions isolated under `services/`.

## Suggested Frontend Structure

```txt
src/
  screens/
  components/
    layout/
    ui/
    checkin/
    timeline/
    insights/
  services/
  hooks/
  types/
  constants/
  utils/
```

## Core Screens

- HomeScreen: quick entry point with warm greeting and “Start check-in”.
- CheckInScreen: pain, stiffness, energy, fatigue, swelling, notes.
- JointPickerScreen: simple joint chips first; SVG body map can come later.
- TimelineScreen: daily history and pain trend.
- InsightsScreen: simple explanation cards.

## Core Data Types

Use these concepts:

- Patient
- PatientBaseline
- DailyCheckin
- DailyJointSymptom
- JointReference
- DailyExposure
- IngestionEvent
- IngestionPerception
- MedicationPlan
- MedicationAdherenceEvent
- WeatherObservation
- InsightCard

DailyCheckin is the central frontend object.

Minimum useful check-in fields:

- entryDate
- checkinTimestamp
- painLevel
- stiffnessLevel
- energyLevel
- fatigueLevel
- swellingPresent
- notesText
- jointSymptoms

## API Direction

Early frontend-facing endpoints:

```txt
GET    /patients
POST   /patients
GET    /patients/{patientId}
PATCH  /patients/{patientId}

POST   /patients/{patientId}/checkins
GET    /patients/{patientId}/checkins
GET    /checkins/{checkinId}
PATCH  /checkins/{checkinId}

GET    /joints
POST   /checkins/{checkinId}/joint-symptoms
GET    /checkins/{checkinId}/joint-symptoms

GET    /patients/{patientId}/insights
```

Do not invent unrelated endpoints.

## UI Style

The app should feel:

- warm
- simple
- calm
- human
- readable
- supportive

Avoid:

- hospital dashboard feel
- dense forms
- tiny text
- scary warnings
- technical labels
- complicated charts

## Language Rules

Use plain, gentle copy.

Good examples:

- “How do you feel today?”
- “Pain looks higher than usual this week.”
- “This may be related to poor sleep.”
- “We need more days before showing stronger patterns.”

Avoid:

- “Causal inference”
- “Prediction model”
- “Clinical recommendation”
- “Abnormal symptom event”

## Medical Safety

Never tell the patient to start, stop, increase, or decrease medication.
Never diagnose.
Never claim that a trigger caused a flare.
Use “may be related” language.

## Coding Rules

- Prefer simple readable code.
- Use TypeScript types.
- Keep components small.
- Prefer reusable UI components only when reuse is clear.
- Do not add major packages without a clear reason.
- Use mock data when backend status is unclear.
- Do not block MVP progress on advanced features like SVG joint maps or wearables.

